# build_jishi.tcl -- one-shot synthesis + PnR + bitstream for the JISHI board
#
# * NORMALLY YOU SHOULD NOT RUN THIS FILE DIRECTLY. Run the wrapper instead:
#     powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File bringup\jishi\build_jishi.ps1
#   The wrapper:
#     (1) generates a tiny Tcl in build/ with the ABSOLUTE project path baked in,
#     (2) runs it, then
#     (3) copies the four reports you must read into bringup/jishi/evidence/.
#
# !! WHY THE ABSOLUTE PATH MATTERS (2026-09-17 fix -- this was a real bug):
#   The first version of this script did `open_project bringup/jishi/jishi.gprj` (relative).
#   That only works if gw_sh happens to be launched from the repository root.
#   Run it from anywhere else and Tcl cannot find the project -> the flow fails, and the
#   failure looks like a *tool* problem rather than a *path* problem.
#   => A script meant to run "from anywhere" must not contain a path that depends on the
#      caller's working directory. (Same family as C19: layer/quoting assumptions.)
#
# This file therefore only documents the flow. The generated Tcl looks like:
#
#   open_project <ABSOLUTE REPO>/bringup/jishi/jishi.gprj
#   set_option -top_module jishi_top
#   run all
#   exit
#
# WHY "run all" (and not just synthesis):
#   It gives the full chain -- synthesis, Place & Route, timing analysis, bitstream --
#   with no board attached. This project has already been bitten by
#   "simulation passes != synthesis OK", so the toolchain itself is evidence.
#
# TWO WARNINGS (both are lessons this repo already paid for):
#   * Do NOT run this while the Gowin IDE has the project open.
#   * Close the IDE before editing .gprj / .cst / .sdc.
#
# (This file is intentionally kept as documentation; the executable copy is generated by
#  build_jishi.ps1 into build/jishi_build.tcl so that no absolute path is ever committed.)
